WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.142
A Spark application usually has a driver and one or more executors.

00:00:05.142 --> 00:00:07.867
Think of the driver as the coordinator.

00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:13.922
It understands the job, builds the execution plan, and coordinates the work.

00:00:13.922 --> 00:00:16.912
Think of the executors as the workers.

00:00:16.912 --> 00:00:21.939
They process partitions of the data and perform the tasks assigned to them.

00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:25.473
Suppose we have a very large sales dataset.

00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:31.488
Instead of one process reading every row, Spark can divide the data into partitions and let several executors work at

00:00:31.488 --> 00:00:34.843
the same time.

00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:41.833
One important warning is that we normally do not want to bring a huge distributed dataset back to the driver.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:46.980
Doing that can create a memory bottleneck and defeats the purpose of distributing the work.
